WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.127
Chunking is one of the most important design decisions in a RAG system.

00:00:04.127 --> 00:00:10.662
If we embed an entire long document, the vector may represent many unrelated ideas at once.

00:00:10.662 --> 00:00:19.442
Retrieval might find the right document but still send a large amount of irrelevant context to the model.

00:00:19.442 --> 00:00:21.672
If chunks are too small, we create the opposite problem.

00:00:21.672 --> 00:00:30.372
A retrieved passage may contain the right sentence but lose an important condition or explanation that

00:00:30.372 --> 00:00:32.502
appeared just before it. [tts-break]

00:00:32.502 --> 00:00:38.167
Overlap helps reduce boundary problems by repeating a small amount of text between neighboring chunks.

00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:42.892
There is no universal best chunk size.

00:00:42.892 --> 00:00:49.012
A legal policy, a support article, a research paper, and source code may need different strategies.

00:00:49.012 --> 00:00:55.157
For this module, we begin with simple word-based chunks because the logic is easy to see.

00:00:55.157 --> 00:00:58.997
Later, you can move to token-aware, sentence-aware, section-aware,

00:00:58.997 --> 00:01:02.960
or semantic chunking when the project requires it.
